# Learning Basilisk

A guide to using the Basilisk flow solver and to the mathematics behind it.

Anil Kumar, Indian Institute of Technology Bombay, Mumbai.

This guide teaches Basilisk in three parts. The first part is practical: the Basilisk C language and its event system, how to set up, run, restart and post-process a case, four complete worked examples (a lid-driven cavity, a vortex street behind a cylinder, a rising bubble and a collapsing water column) and the mistakes that most often break a simulation. The second part explains the numerical method, from the CFD ideas it rests on (finite volumes, stability and the CFL condition, upwinding, implicit time stepping, projection methods) to each step of the incompressible Navier--Stokes solver: the adaptive grid, Bell--Colella--Glaz advection, momentum-conserving transport for two fluids, implicit viscosity, balanced-force gravity and surface tension, the pressure projection and its multigrid solver, geometric volume-of-fluid advection, ghost-cell boundary conditions, cut cells for solid bodies, and wave-generating and absorbing layers. Each derivation is followed by the source line in [src/](/src/) that computes it. The third part surveys the other solvers in the library.

> **Note on how this guide was written.** This documentation was generated with the help of Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic [[15](references)]. Claude drafted text, derivations, figures and example programs from the Basilisk source code under the author's direction. Mistakes may remain; if you find one, please tell the author through the [Basilisk forum](https://groups.google.com/g/basilisk-fr).

